member.remarks:
The angle is first wrapped into a single turn, (-180°, 180°], so 450° reads as 90° and -270° as 90° too. A text
turned more than a right angle past upright, plus the tolerance, reads upside down and is turned a half turn;
one that leans less is left as it is.

remarks.para:
This was the one reading held back on purpose. Every other question a chain answers walks its edges once;
this one walks every pair of edges, so it grows with the two edge counts multiplied and not added — two
forty-edge bars are sixteen hundred edge pairs, each of them an arc against an arc.

member.param:
The reach of each plane in , in its order: a plane is read only for the cells whose box
comes within the tolerance of it, the cells it was put to (see
);
null where every plane was put to every cell.

member.summary:
The point tolerance below which a boolean's cut of openings that comes out open is cut again (see
): a
thousandth, the default.

remarks.para:
This is the net body of a part of a model, cut by every part it meets: of the 79 864 parts of a Tekla model so
cut within a thousandth, 180 had a cut skipped, 323 cuts in all; with a contact of a hundredth, 109 and 196;
with a fallback of a hundredth as well, 19 and 38, and 136 cuts were taken within the fallback.

member.summary:
Determines whether a face of some fills crosses a face of the body as filled so far, a face it was given or one of
a fill taken before: an edge of the one passing through the inside of the other, either way round (see
); each face added is set only against the faces whose boxes meet its own.

Finds a face new to a body lying back to back with another, or another with it, as  does;
and, where asked, a face new to it crossing another, an edge of either passing through the inside of the other, as
 reads two faces.

member.returns:
true when the ray starts on the plane within the planar tolerance, or reaches it as
 reads it;
otherwise, false.

member.summary:
Gathers the faces standing that a point may stand above by a height or more, and perhaps a few more, in no order;
false, and every face to be read, while they are few. The cells are laid out the first time they are read, and
faces added since the last time are filed first.

member.returns:
Where an edge of either crosses the other's material, the first face's edges first, each place named once;
where the two lie in one plane, where their rims cross, in the order of
.

remarks.para:
The cut runs from the origin to the point at reach R = max(0, t) + max(1, 100 · EqualPoint), where t is an upper
bound of how far along the ray any point of the shape projects. For a straight shape t is taken over its corners;
for a circle, an arc or an ellipse it is the centre brought onto the ray plus the radius, or the major radius.

member.summary:
Finds how the surfaces of two balls meet. The answer is worked out from the ball that comes first (see
), so that asked either way round it is the same to the bit, but for the normal of the circle,
which points from the ball asked towards the other.

member.remarks:
Taken without filtering, so the walk reads no tolerance: of a triangle with three corners apart it is the
polygon of them, the one every other question asks; of one of no width it runs along the segment and back;
and of one point it stays there, where a polygon or a chain of distinct corners could not be built at all.

The rule is for lines parallel by rounding, a sine of 1E-9 or less, such as a line rebuilt from two points of another;
lines at any real angle cross, whatever the tolerance, even where it is 1E-12 or nought. With a point tolerance of a
thousandth and coordinates near nought the limit is 4.5E12 along. Two rays are read by it as well (see ).

Two lines in space meet only where they happen to, and are otherwise skew: as far apart as their common
perpendicular is long. They have one unless they are parallel by rounding: a sine below 1E-15, or a sine of 1E-9 or
less with the perpendicular further out than a double can place within the point tolerance (see
); a NaN has none either. It is worked out from the one that comes first (see
), so that asked either way round the gap is the same to the bit.

member.param:
The area of the four-sided figures each pair that cancelled makes with its ends, together; nought when none
cancelled. One edge run both ways by the two faces sharing it leaves the rounding of the points a cut put on it;
two edges a hair apart, the two sides of a sliver, leave the sliver.

member.summary:
The array the DXF is written in and its length, with no copy of it where the array is not much longer than the DXF;
the bytes past the length are not DXF. Where a quarter of the array or more is unused it is cut to its length, for
the drawing that is opened from it holds the array as long as it is open.

Reads the header variables from the data of their section. From R2007 the data start with their size in bits;
the texts are UTF-16 in a string stream at their end and the handles follow them, as in an object. Throws
 where the data end before the last of them.

The name of a shape in the file of shapes of its text style (group 3), that file looked for in the support folders
in their order, by its name alone and with .shx where it has no extension, or where its style gives it whole;
null where no folder holds a compiled file of shapes of that name, or it names no shape of that number.

remarks.para:
The value sets are taken as AutoCAD 2021 took them: of a Length from 50 to 300 by 10, 75 goes to 70 and 400 to 300;
of an Angle in steps of 15 degrees, 40 goes to 45 and -10 to 345; of a Length of 50, 100, 150 or 200 for the block's
own 100, 75 and 125 go to 100 and 175 to 150, from every value before.

